WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Speaker 1: I have incredible economic&nbsp;
data to start today with. And I tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:12.840
these jobs numbers, not because they are the&nbsp;
proof that everything Joe Biden has done has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:19.080
correct. These numbers are not proof that Biden&nbsp;
has fixed the systemic and structural issues with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.480
our economy, which include inequality and so many&nbsp;
other things which we talk about all the time. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.540
give you these numbers so that you understand&nbsp;
that by the same metrics that Republicans use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:38.460
to judge their presidents, the economy is doing&nbsp;
fine. And now fine doesn't mean there will never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:46.560
be a recession. Fine doesn't mean some people are&nbsp;
unemployed. Fine means there is no great alarm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:56.100
sounding of an imminent Biden induced recession.&nbsp;
Let's first talk about the data. 497,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:03.480
jobs added in June. You might say, well, I don't&nbsp;
know, 300, but 4335 million people in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:10.860
States is 497,000 new jobs in June. Remarkable.&nbsp;
It's remarkable in the sense that the estimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:17.940
was that there would only be 220,000 jobs added&nbsp;
in June. And the real number, 497,000 is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:25.200
than double that. More than double that. This&nbsp;
is also significantly more than the 267,000 jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:34.200
that were added in May. The leading industries&nbsp;
were leisure and hospitality. Some say leisure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.420
I know people who say leisure and hospitality.&nbsp;
Either way, these are jobs paid in U.S. dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.360
no matter what pronunciation you use. 232,000&nbsp;
of that for 97 was leisure and hospitality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:51.900
nearly 100,000 jobs in construction, trade,&nbsp;
transportation and utilities at 90,000. Also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:59.160
interesting when it comes to construction, this&nbsp;
is an industry that when you talk to two random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:06.780
people who were supporters of MAGA in Trump's&nbsp;
first term, they would say construction is booming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:14.100
under Trump. And we really think construction&nbsp;
is an important leading economic indicator. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.440
you see more construction jobs, you know that in&nbsp;
the future, if it's commercial offices that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.760
being built, those will eventually be filled with&nbsp;
tenants. So that's a leading indicator for a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.080
economy when you're talking about houses, single&nbsp;
family homes and condos. When you see construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.820
jobs, it means single family houses and condos are&nbsp;
being built. And thus eventually when those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.500
those are built, they will sell and you will have&nbsp;
people buying them. These are leading indicators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:44.040
they said under Trump, we are seeing construction&nbsp;
jobs growing significantly. So what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.320
takeaway here? The takeaway is not one about&nbsp;
we now have a perfect economy. We've solved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.700
every problem we've ever had when it comes to&nbsp;
wages and employment and all of these different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:59.520
things. The point here is that the right wing&nbsp;
media and politicians are trying to spread a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.880
false narrative. That narrative is that the Biden&nbsp;
economy is terrible and it's because of Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:11.040
and it is Joe Biden's policies that have hurt the&nbsp;
American people. And it's increasingly difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.660
to do this. We played a clip for you a few shows&nbsp;
ago of Larry Kudlow, a former Trump economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:21.600
adviser appearing on Fox News and being asked by&nbsp;
the Fox host. Listen. The setup was something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.340
go check it out for the exact wording, but it&nbsp;
was something like we see all of these economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:32.640
indicators. They look so good. But explain like,&nbsp;
why is the economy actually not so good? And Larry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.720
Kudlow was unable to do so. He said something&nbsp;
about AI and technology. It was pathetic. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.160
failed to make that case. And so what Republicans&nbsp;
in the right have been relegated to is cherry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:48.600
picking data, ignoring context, distorting facts,&nbsp;
ignoring history to try to undermine what is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:56.040
very solid economy that happens to be taking place&nbsp;
when Joe Biden is president and is doing some good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.920
things. It's really important to remember, you&nbsp;
know, if you watch shows that play politics with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.300
this economic data, you can go and find them.&nbsp;
I don't know which they are. I certainly don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:12.540
watch them. But you can go and find those shows.&nbsp;
When we talk about economic data and we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:19.320
about economies in general, I try to be crystal&nbsp;
clear with you that most of what we see is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.500
directly related to what the president does. For&nbsp;
example, inflation is coming down in just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.960
every Western, wealthy, industrialized nation,&nbsp;
whatever term you want to use, what we call the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.720
OECD countries, inflation has been coming down&nbsp;
in all of those countries. So it's not because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.000
Biden that inflation is coming down, but inflation&nbsp;
has been coming down slightly faster in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.320
States than in some of those other countries.&nbsp;
And we can certainly point to some Biden policies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.780
like the Inflation Reduction Act, which are&nbsp;
influencing that inflation would be coming down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:59.520
anyway. But we can argue that the Biden policy,&nbsp;
Biden nomics is helping that along. Similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:07.800
job creation. We are seeing job creation in many&nbsp;
Western OECD countries. But we can say that what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:14.940
Joe Biden has done is making that job creation a&nbsp;
little more robust than if we had, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:21.000
Trump policy in place. It's also really important&nbsp;
to remember when we look at the economy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.880
economies don't perfectly overlap in terms of&nbsp;
their movements with presidential terms. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.820
true that if you look back at the last hundred&nbsp;
years or hundred and 20 years, it is true that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.380
we tend to see higher job creation when there's a&nbsp;
Democrat in the White House. We tend to see better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.460
stock market performance when a Democrat is in&nbsp;
the White House. We see lower unemployment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:45.780
a Democrat is in the White House. We see lower&nbsp;
inflation when a Democrat is in the White House.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:51.840
We see higher GDP when a Democrat is in the White&nbsp;
House. These are averages, but it is not perfect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:58.020
It is not perfectly aligned. And so we had Joe&nbsp;
Biden coming in during the COVID 19 recovery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:06.060
We had the American rescue plan put in place. All&nbsp;
of these different things and the timing of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.760
economic movements will not perfectly align. For&nbsp;
example, we started to see gas prices go up at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.980
the end of Trump's term. They continued going up&nbsp;
during Joe Biden. Then they started coming down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:22.860
Is one or the other specifically to blame for&nbsp;
those movements? No. And so those are the types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:28.020
of political games I try to avoid playing&nbsp;
with you. The other thing is it's important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:33.540
to look more broadly at some of the other&nbsp;
macroeconomic things that are going on. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:39.540
these are going on globally. The gloomy picture&nbsp;
that the right is trying to paint of the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.460
economy not only has to be done by removing&nbsp;
all context from the United States economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.100
but it also has to be done by ignoring broadly&nbsp;
what is happening again in the western, wealthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:56.160
industrialized OECD countries. They are all doing&nbsp;
relatively well, and that makes it even harder to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.740
believe. Data aside, right, if you ignore the&nbsp;
data that says things are looking okay, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:07.980
harder to believe that things are uniquely bad in&nbsp;
the U.S. when we see a generally positive trend in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:12.300
most of these Western economies. So we're going to&nbsp;
continue following these numbers. The bottom line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:17.640
is this is not a terrible economy. It's a pretty&nbsp;
good economy. It's interesting that Joe Biden has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:23.520
been able to actually get done as much as he has&nbsp;
given the level of opposition that Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:30.240
have put forward. And I would actually go further.&nbsp;
The economy would likely be even better were it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:35.460
not for the obstruction of Republicans, many&nbsp;
of whom voted against the things that Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:40.380
tried to do and has been able to do. But now what&nbsp;
credit for them, as we talked about last week.

